<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Lab 4, Due: November 13, 2026</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <term>Instructions:</term> Complete the following exercises in your
            groups.
            For the write up:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        You should type up your report (a google docs version is
                        available on Blackboard).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Include screenshots of your work in Sage.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You only need to hand in one report for your group.
                        Only include the names of students who contributed.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        This lab is graded out of 20 points.
                        Each problem will be graded out of 10 points - you earn
                        5 points by including your reasoning, following all
                        directions, and making only minor errors.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            In this lab, we are going to study the luminance values in an
            <m>8\times 8</m> block of pixels from a digital photo taken in
            Capitol Reef National Park.
        </p>

        <image source="images/lab4_figure1.png" width="75%">
            <shortdescription>
                Luminance values in an 8 by 8 block of pixels from a
                digital photo taken in Capitol Reef National Park.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
            To begin, we'll consider the first column of luminance values, which
            is the 8-dimensional vector <m>\xvec</m> represented graphically as:
        </p>

        <image source="images/lab4_figure2.png" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>
                The first column of luminance values and its graphical
                representation.
            </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
            Two things are important:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        There are not rapid variations in these numbers within
                        the column.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        If some of the values were to change slightly, our eyes
                        would probably not notice because the pixels, when
                        represented on a screen, are so small.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            We have encountered the Fourier basis consisting of vectors
            <m>\vvec_0, \vvec_1, \cdots, \vvec_7</m> and the vector
            <m>\fvec = \{\xvec\}_B</m> consisting of the Fourier coefficients
            <m>F_0, \cdots, F_7</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                There is a page of Sage cells available
                <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/0RF" visual="http://gvsu.edu/s/0RF">here</url>.
                Be sure to evaluate the first cell and then use the rest of the
                cells to compute your work.
                This cell will load the <m>{8\times 8}</m> matrix <m>P</m>,
                whose columns are the vector
                <m>\vvec_0, \vvec_1, \cdots, \vvec_7</m>, as well as the vector
                <m>\xvec</m> shown above and the <m>8\times 8</m> matrix of
                luminance values called <c>luminance</c>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe in words how to convert the vector <m>\xvec</m>
                    into the vector <m>\fvec</m> of Fourier coefficients, and
                    conversely, how to convert <m>\fvec</m> back into
                    <m>\xvec</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the vector <m>\fvec</m>, take a screenshot of it, and
                    paste it below.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What does the relative size of the Fourier coefficients tell
                    us about the relative contributions of the basis vectors
                    <m>\vvec_0, \vvec_1, \cdots, \vvec_7</m> to the vector
                    <m>\xvec</m>?
                    How is your observation consistent with the fact that "there
                    are not rapid variations" in the vector <m>\xvec</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now form a new vector <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>, which
                    is formed from <m>\fvec</m> by rounding the Fourier
                    coefficients to the nearest integers and setting the last
                    two Fourier coefficients to zero.
                    In other words, we are only saving 6 of the 8 Fourier
                    coefficients or <m>75\%</m> of them.
                    Find <m>\xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>, an approximation to the
                    vector <m>\xvec</m>, formed by the approximate Fourier
                    coefficients <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>.
                    Describe the vector <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m> and how
                    you found <m>\xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>.
                    Paste a screenshot of <m>\xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m> below.
                    (Remember that you can define a vector as
                    <c>v = vector([2, 1])</c>.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is <m>\xvec - \xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m>, which
                    measures the error in your approximation?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Here's the central idea: we have saved only <m>75\%</m> of
                    the Fourier coefficients, but when we reconstruct the visual
                    information in the vector <m>\xvec</m>, the error is pretty
                    small and probably not visible to the human eye (remember
                    that the components of <m>\xvec</m> can vary between 0 and
                    225).
                    We say that the <em>compression ratio</em> is <m>75\%</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Let's try again saving only the four largest Fourier
                    coefficients (a compression ration of <m>50\%</m> ); that
                    is, form a new <m>\fvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m> by setting the
                    four smallest Fourier coefficients to zero.
                    What is the error <m>\xvec - \xvec_{\textrm{approx}}</m> in
                    this case?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        Notice the trade-off: we are only saving <m>50\%</m> of the Fourier
        coefficients, but the error in the reconstructed vector <m>\xvec</m> is
        bigger.
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                We can, in fact, do better!
                Let's return to the <m>8\times 8</m> block of luminance values
                from the beginning.
                Each column of luminance values forms an 8-dimensional vector,
                the first of which we studied in the previous section.
                If <m>\xvec_i</m> is the i <m>^{\textrm{th}}</m> column of the
                luminance matrix, then we find its corresponding vector
                <m>\fvec_i</m> of Fourier coefficients.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the <m>8\times 8</m> matrix whose columns are the
                    vectors <m>\fvec_0, \fvec_1, \cdots, \fvec_7</m> and paste a
                    screenshot of your results in the space below.
                    Include a description of how you found this matrix.
                    This may sound daunting at first, but remember how
                    matrix-matrix multiplication works and that you have the
                    <m>8\times 8</m> matrix <c>luminance</c> of luminance values
                    available on your page of Sage cells.
                </p>

                <p>
                    As you scan across a row, you'll see that the values don't
                    change wildly.
                    So, maybe we can Fourier transform the <em>rows</em> of the
                    matrix Fourier coefficients.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Define the 8-dimensional vector <m>\yvec</m> consisting of
                    the rounded values of the first row of the Fourier
                    coefficient matrix and then find its Fourier coefficients
                    <m>\{\yvec\}_B</m>.
                    Paste a screenshot of the <m>\{\yvec\}_B</m> below.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do you notice about the relative size of these Fourier
                    coefficients?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    This suggests that we can Fourier transform each of the rows
                    of the Fourier coefficient matrix.
                    This is called the <em>double Fourier transform</em>, and
                    there's a function <c>double_fourier(luminance)</c> that
                    does this from the original luminance values.
                    Give the resulting matrix a name and paste a screenshot of
                    it below.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do you notice about the size of the entries of this
                    matrix?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    There is another command
                    <c>round_matrix(matrix_name, minimum)</c> that will round
                    the entries in the given matrix to the nearest integer and
                    set any entries whose absolute value is smaller than
                    <c>minimum</c> to zero.
                    For instance, <c>round_matrix(A, 4)</c> will round the
                    entries of <m>A</m> and set any entries in the range from -3
                    to 3 equal to zero.
                    Round the matrix of double Fourier coefficients with a
                    minimum value of 4 and paste your result below.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many nonzero double Fourier coefficients are left?
                    These are the only ones that we need to save so the ratio of
                    the number of nonzero double Fourier coefficients to 64
                    represents the compression ratio.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    There is a final command
                    <c>inverse_double_fourier(matrix_name)</c> that will
                    reconstruct the luminance from the rounded double Fourier
                    coefficients.
                    Find this reconstruction and paste it below.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the error between the original luminance values and
                    the reconstructed values?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        Notice that we have only saved about <m>20\%</m> of the Fourier
        coefficients yet we are able to reconstruct the luminance values with a
        relatively small error.
        When you take a picture with your phone, the software saves the rounded
        double Fourier coefficients of each <m>8\times 8</m> block of pixels,
        which represents only a small fraction of the information.
        When you want to view a picture, the software reconstructs the image
        from the saved double Fourier coefficients with a relatively small
        error.
        Most photo editing programs allow you to choose the compression ratio,
        which is controlled by the range of Fourier coefficients we save.
    </p>

    <p>
        Physiologists have found that the human eye is more sensitive to the
        luminance values than the chrominance values.
        This means that we're able to throw away more of the Fourier
        coefficients from the chrominance values to achieve an even greater
        savings.
    </p>

    <p>
        Similar ideas are involved in storing movies and digital music.
        Next time you're streaming a show, you will enjoy it even more when you
        realize that lots of Fourier coefficients are being streamed to your
        device.
    </p>
</worksheet>